### Summary

`reclaimSqliteWalFreePages` counts each checkpoint invocation in `checkpointCalls`, but counts incomplete checkpoints only from the final fallback result, omitting earlier calls that returned false.


### Steps to reproduce

1. Use the tested source commit listed below with development dependencies installed.
2. Save the following test as `src/config/sessions/issue-checkpoint-incomplete.test.ts`.
3. Run from the repository root:

```sh
node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run src/config/sessions/issue-checkpoint-incomplete.test.ts
```

The test invokes the real reclamation function with a controlled database boundary and checkpoint callback. It covers TRUNCATE=false/PASSIVE=true and TRUNCATE=false/PASSIVE=false, plus controls for timeout restoration and checkpoint mode.

```ts
import type { DatabaseSync } from "node:sqlite";
import { expect, test } from "vitest";
import { reclaimSqliteWalFreePages } from "../../infra/sqlite-wal-reclamation.js";

type FakeDatabase = {
  db: DatabaseSync;
  execCalls: string[];
  preparedCalls: string[];
  timeout: () => number;
};

function createFakeDatabase(initialTimeoutMs = 5_000): FakeDatabase {
  let timeoutMs = initialTimeoutMs;
  const execCalls: string[] = [];
  const preparedCalls: string[] = [];
  const db = {
    get isOpen() {
      return true;
    },
    prepare(sql: string) {
      preparedCalls.push(sql);
      if (sql === "PRAGMA busy_timeout") {
        return { get: () => ({ busy_timeout: timeoutMs }) };
      }
      if (sql.includes("freelist_count")) {
        return { get: () => ({ freelist_count: 0 }) };
      }
      throw new Error(`unexpected SQL in audit fake: ${sql}`);
    },
    exec(sql: string) {
      execCalls.push(sql);
      const match = /^PRAGMA busy_timeout = (\d+)$/u.exec(sql);
      if (match) {
        timeoutMs = Number(match[1]);
      }
    },
  } as unknown as DatabaseSync;
  return { db, execCalls, preparedCalls, timeout: () => timeoutMs };
}

test("checkpointIncomplete counts every false checkpoint call, including a fallback", () => {
  const fallback = createFakeDatabase();
  const fallbackModes: string[] = [];
  const fallbackResult = reclaimSqliteWalFreePages(
    fallback.db,
    (mode) => {
      fallbackModes.push(mode);
      return mode === "PASSIVE";
    },
    {},
  );
  const blocked = createFakeDatabase();
  const blockedResult = reclaimSqliteWalFreePages(
    blocked.db,
    () => false,
    {},
  );

  expect({
    fallback: {
      modes: fallbackModes,
      checkpointCalls: fallbackResult.checkpointCalls,
      checkpointIncomplete: fallbackResult.checkpointIncomplete,
      checkpointCompleted: fallbackResult.checkpointCompleted,
    },
    blocked: {
      checkpointCalls: blockedResult.checkpointCalls,
      checkpointIncomplete: blockedResult.checkpointIncomplete,
      checkpointCompleted: blockedResult.checkpointCompleted,
    },
  }).toEqual({
    fallback: {
      modes: ["TRUNCATE", "PASSIVE"],
      checkpointCalls: 2,
      checkpointIncomplete: 1,
      checkpointCompleted: true,
    },
    blocked: {
      checkpointCalls: 2,
      checkpointIncomplete: 2,
      checkpointCompleted: false,
    },
  });
});

test("timing fields do not change the checkpoint busy timeout", () => {
  const fake = createFakeDatabase(5_000);
  const result = reclaimSqliteWalFreePages(fake.db, () => true, {});
  expect(result.checkpointCompleted).toBe(true);
  expect(fake.execCalls).toEqual(["PRAGMA busy_timeout = 0", "PRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000"]);
  expect(fake.timeout()).toBe(5_000);
});

test("timing fields do not change the checkpoint mode", () => {
  const defaultModes: string[] = [];
  reclaimSqliteWalFreePages(
    createFakeDatabase().db,
    (mode) => {
      defaultModes.push(mode);
      return true;
    },
    {},
  );
  expect(defaultModes).toEqual(["TRUNCATE"]);

  const overriddenModes: string[] = [];
  reclaimSqliteWalFreePages(
    createFakeDatabase().db,
    (mode) => {
      overriddenModes.push(mode);
      return true;
    },
    { checkpointMode: "FULL" },
  );
  expect(overriddenModes).toEqual(["FULL"]);
});

### Expected behavior

checkpointIncomplete` counts every checkpoint call returning false, as documented in `docs/logging.md:832-836`.

| Checkpoint results | checkpointCalls | checkpointIncomplete | checkpointCompleted |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| TRUNCATE=false, PASSIVE=true | 2 | 1 | true |
| TRUNCATE=false, PASSIVE=false | 2 | 2 | false |

[Documented counter semantics](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/fc23bc864e4553c2d215e479eeec47b67a0bf943/docs/logging.md#L832-L836).

### Actual behavior


| Checkpoint results | checkpointCalls | checkpointIncomplete | checkpointCompleted |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| TRUNCATE=false, PASSIVE=true | 2 | 0 | true |
| TRUNCATE=false, PASSIVE=false | 2 | 1 | false |

The first incomplete call is omitted from both results. Successful fallback completely hides that false return from `checkpointIncomplete`.


### OpenClaw version

v2026.9.8, source commit `fc23bc864e4553c2d215e479eeec47b67a0bf943`.

### Operating system

Ubuntu 22.04 under WSL2 on Windows; Node.js v24.18.0; Vitest 5.0.1.

### Install method

Source checkout with development dependencies installed; executed through the repository test runner.

### Model

Not applicable; no LLM inference is performed.

### Provider / routing chain

Not applicable; local SQLite reclamation diagnostics without a model provider.

### Additional provider/model setup details

The production `reclaimSqliteWalFreePages` function is used directly. DatabaseSync is replaced by a controlled test object for the relevant PRAGMA operations; the injected checkpoint callback supplies deterministic true/false results. This reproduces the counting defect without creating a real database lock.


### Logs

```shell
false -> true:
  checkpointCalls:      expected 2, received 2
  checkpointIncomplete: expected 1, received 0
  checkpointCompleted:  expected true, received true

false -> false:
  checkpointCalls:      expected 2, received 2
  checkpointIncomplete: expected 2, received 1
  checkpointCompleted:  expected false, received false

Tests: 1 failed / 2 passed
```

### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

Original-source runs repeatedly failed the counter assertion. A candidate correction passed all three tests; restoring the original source restored the failure. A separate rerun of the original source also produced 1 failed / 2 passed. The timeout-restoration and mode controls passed.

[Counting implementation](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/fc23bc864e4553c2d215e479eeec47b67a0bf943/src/infra/sqlite-wal-reclamation.ts#L65-L77).


### Impact and severity

Affected: consumers of SQLite reclamation diagnostic counters when TRUNCATE returns false and triggers PASSIVE fallback.

Severity: low; incomplete checkpoint attempts are underreported, making the diagnostics less reliable.

Frequency: both deterministic fallback cases reproduce the undercount, including repeated original-source runs.

The demonstrated impact is diagnostic accuracy. Database corruption, data loss, and a failed checkpoint recovery were not observed in this reproduction.

### Additional information

At the tested commit, `checkpointCalls` is incremented after each callback invocation, while `checkpointIncomplete += Number(!completed)` runs only once after fallback. Counting each false result separately resolves the demonstrated mismatch while retaining the final `checkpointCompleted` status. The tested candidate correction was restored and has not been merged. No known-good version has been established.

