Closes #200075

## What Problem This Solves

Deleted cron-session archives can inflate the eligible session count and leave `memory status` reporting pending indexing after ordinary conversations are already indexed.

## User Impact

Internal cron archives no longer create pending memory work or enter the conversation index. Ordinary deleted conversations remain searchable.

## Why This Change Was Made

Archive admission now recognizes cron base keys as well as per-run keys, using the existing cron classifier through the lightweight session-key SDK. Live-session provenance rules are preserved.

## Evidence

- Reproduced with real SQLite session creation, transcript writes and deletion archives, then verified with the built CLI in isolated FTS-only state:

  | Same archived corpus | Eligible sessions | Indexed files | Dirty |
  | --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
  | Before | 2 | 1 | `true` |
  | After, before reindexing | 1 | 1 | `false` |
  | After indexing | 1 | 1 | `false` |

- CLI search retains the ordinary conversation and returns no results for either cron-base or cron-run archive content.
- The deletion-lifecycle regression fails on the base for cron-base admission and passes with the fix. All 47 focused tests across five files pass, including live cron-base user content and retrieval import isolation.
- The changed lifecycle suite has 16 cases with 34.38 seconds of test execution; the three-file extension run takes 79.10 seconds including compilation. It exercises persisted deletion archives, listener admission and startup discovery.
- `pnpm build`, targeted TypeScript and lint checks, formatting, and plugin import-boundary checks pass.
- Independent Codex agent review of the complete three-file diff found no actionable issues.

