Related: #198531

## What Problem This Solves

Concurrent `git log -S` and `git blame` history reads can start overlapping blob fetches into one shared object store when an older shim's `openclaw-history-blobs.lock` marker is present, and a leftover marker whose pid was reused by an unrelated live process makes every content search wait on it forever.

## User Impact

Agents in linked worktrees share one history-prefetch lock: one fetch runs at a time, and a stale legacy marker costs at most 30 s per invocation instead of blocking every later search. The bounded wait now runs before the kernel lock, so N parallel callers wait 30 s together rather than k x 30 s in lock order. When the wait expires, that invocation skips the prefetch: non-show content searches run offline and exit 2 with the budget guidance, while `show` keeps its speculative native fallback. The marker is never removed, so the 30 s cost repeats while the pid lives. There is no user-visible UI change.

## Why This Change Was Made

`prepare_blobs` serialised prefetches with a `mkdir` directory lock plus an owner `pid` file. That protocol removes and recreates the lock pathname: a waiter that decides the owner is dead deletes the record and the directory, so it can delete the lock a successor has just created and two callers can then fetch into the same object store at once. A leftover `pid` value that now belongs to a live process also resets the attempt counter forever. In the base, a live old owner reset that counter and the wait had no bound.

The lock is now a kernel lock on a file the shim never unlinks. The open and the lock run in a nested subshell so a failed `exec` cannot end the caller and the fallback stays reachable:

```sh
if ! (
  { exec 9>>"$common/openclaw-history-blobs.lock.kernel"; } 2>/dev/null || exit 1
  case $(uname -s) in
    Linux) flock -x 9 2>/dev/null || exit 1 ;;
    *)     lockf -s 9 2>/dev/null || exit 1 ;;
  esac
  ...
); then
  # mkdir fallback, no reclaim
fi
```

The kernel lock file stays in `.git` as an empty file; it is never removed. With no unlink there is no reclaim step and no interval in which two callers both believe they own the lock. The descriptor is opened inside the `prepare_blobs` subshell, so the kernel releases the lock when the subshell exits and no cleanup trap is needed.

An old shim cannot see the kernel lock: an old process that starts while a new prefetch is running can still create its directory marker and fetch beside it. The upgrade window is one-sided. The new side reads the old marker before taking the kernel lock and serves a live owner first; that orders the common case without changing processes that are already running. A live old owner with a large batch used to wait without bound; the new side ends the wait after 30 s, skips its own prefetch, and leaves the old owner to finish.

Because `kill -0` only shows that a pid exists, the legacy wait keeps the 300-iteration `sleep 0.1` bound the directory lock had. It runs before the kernel lock, so parallel callers time out together instead of queueing for another 30 s each. A timed-out caller skips the prefetch; `prepared_blobs` is already set for non-show content searches, so they take the failed-preparation path (offline, exit 2, budget error) rather than starting a lazy fetch beside the old owner. `show` keeps the speculative native fallback that #198531 gives it.

If `flock` or `lockf -s` is missing, or the platform rejects the descriptor form, the shim falls back to an `mkdir` mutex without the old reclaim step. Cleanup removes the record only while the process still owns the pid file, so it cannot delete a successor's lock. If the fallback owner is killed before cleanup, the directory stays permanently; every later prefetch on that path waits 30 s and skips until it is removed. The directory lives in the git-common-dir, not in a linked worktree's `.git` file. Stop every process that may use it, then remove it with `rm -r "$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/openclaw-history-blobs.lock.fallback"`. This keeps a clean system working instead of turning every content search into exit 2. The `lockf -s fd` form exists in FreeBSD since December 2023; the minimum macOS version is UNKNOWN, and older userlands take the fallback.

The kernel lock and the fallback mkdir mutex are different paths. Two environments that share a `.git` but resolve different binaries, for example a container with a bind mount and its host, do not exclude each other; the old directory marker is the only cross-environment signal.

Scope: two files, +450/-23. The shim replaces the twenty-line reclaim protocol with the kernel lock, the portable fallback, and the bounded legacy read; the history test adds the stale-marker race, the fallback cases, the release-before-limit path, and the updated legacy timeout. The reproduction script stays out of the diff and is in the collapsed block below.

## Evidence

Environment: ephemeral Ubuntu 26.04.1 sandbox (kernel 6.12.114, node v24.22.3, git 2.53.0). Base `22614d5b0e81fc68cbbbf6bdcdd34301a3e02658`; patch applied on a clean tree; `git write-tree` = `12ab4e144960048691edd2a96aaaab64ab492b51`.

